# Response headers for every asset this Worker serves.
#
# Cloudflare's asset handler sends none of these by default, and this is the
# first time the app is exposed on a public origin at all.
#
# The CSP is the one that earns its keep: the ranking renders game titles and
# outbound links derived from Reddit and Lemmy posts, so it is the control that
# keeps a future rendering mistake from becoming script execution on the deploy
# origin. React's escaping and the http(s)-only URL constraint in
# `src/corpus/schema.ts` are the primary defences; this is the layer under them.
#
# `style-src` carries no 'unsafe-inline'. The app authors no inline `style`
# attributes and no `<style>` elements — every app rule comes from StyleX. (React
# applies component styles via the CSSOM, which `style-src` does not govern, so a
# design-system component that sets a dynamic inline style is unaffected.) This is
# verified by loading the built site under this exact policy and watching for
# violations, not by reading the source.
#
# `img-src` allows exactly one third-party host, `cdn.cloudflare.steamstatic.com`,
# for game hero images (the Steam store header derived from an entry's Steam
# store link). It is a pinned https host, not a wildcard: an image cannot execute
# script, and pinning one host keeps the strict script-src/style-src posture
# intact while blocking every other origin. The host must match the one the app
# builds its image URLs from (src/app/views/steamImage.ts).
/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self'; img-src 'self' data: https://cdn.cloudflare.steamstatic.com; connect-src 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'none'
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
